    "body": "Amazon told New York City customers on Thursday that a proposed city law could add up to $5 per order. The company said it may consider moving delivery facilities outside the city if the bill passes.\nWhat’s Related\nThe bill would require operators of certain last-mile facilities to get a city license and directly employ workers who sort and handle packages or regularly deliver them to customers, with some exceptions. Amazon told the City Council in April that the bill, as written, would end its contracts with more than 40 local delivery businesses that employ more than 5,000 people.\nCouncilmember Tiffany Cabán introduced the proposal, known as Intro 518 or the Delivery Protection Act, in February. The City Council’s consumer and worker protection committee held a hearing in April, and the bill remains in committee.\nWho it applies to\nAmazon calls the companies that deliver packages from its stations Delivery Service Partners. These businesses hire their own drivers, manage their fleets, and assign routes. Under the bill, operators of covered facilities would generally have to bring that work in-house. The hiring requirement would apply to full-time drivers and part-time drivers who do covered delivery work for more than eight hours in any 30-day period.\nThe direct-hire rules would take effect roughly a year after the bill becomes law. Operators could apply for extensions for qualifying contracts already in place. An extension would end when the contract expires or roughly two years after the bill becomes law, whichever comes first.\nRELATED STORIES: New York City’s Delivery Boom Is Leading to More Crashes and Injuries | Amazon Delivery Vehicles Got Over 14,000 Citations in NYC\nWhat happens to workers if contracts end\nIf the bill forces an operator to end a contract and the contractor lays off workers doing covered work, the operator would have to offer those workers their old jobs as direct employees before hiring anyone else. The offers would have to preserve existing employment terms and benefits.\nThe Teamsters pointed to a recent contract cancellation in calling for the bill’s passage. About 200 Manhattan e-bike delivery workers were affected after Amazon ended its contract with Kaizen Management Solutions this month, Streetsblog reported. Amazon told Streetsblog it ended the contract over safety issues and other breaches and was connecting affected workers with other delivery partners.\nWhat delivery stations would need to do\nEach covered location would need a two-year city license with a $500 annual fee. Operators that continue using contracted drivers under an extension would have to track their routes, verify their insurance, and post a $500,000 bond per contracted driver to cover potential court judgments from road incidents.\nAmazon said moving facilities outside the city would likely mean slower and more expensive deliveries. Its notice did not name any stations it might move or say whether its current delivery partners could keep serving New York City from those locations.",
